1. Enforce Server-Side Source of Truth

In a secure payment integration, the JSON configuration you shared earlier is strictly for displaying options in the UI. When your local client sends a deposit or withdrawal request to your server, your backend code must ignore any pricing or limit rules sent by the client.
• What to verify in your server code:
	• Amount & Limits: Check that the requested amount is a positive number and falls strictly between the server's stored minDepositAmount and maxDepositAmount. Reject negative numbers, zero, or non-numeric strings.
	• Fee Calculation: Calculate feeValue and chargeableFeeValue directly on the server using your own database configuration. Never accept a fee field from the client's JSON request.
	• Currency Locking: Validate that the currencyCode (e.g., XAF) matches the allowed currency for the selected paymentProvider on the backend.

2. Implement Strict Authorization & Ownership Checks

Your local server must ensure that a user can only initiate transactions for their own authenticated account.
• What to verify in your server code:
	• Session-Based Identity: Extract the user's ID from a verified JWT or server-side session rather than trusting a userId or accountId field inside the JSON body.
	• Phone Number / Wallet Binding: If your platform restricts withdrawals to verified numbers, confirm on the server that the target MoMo number belongs to the authenticated user before calling the payment aggregator API.

3. Secure Your Callback / Webhook Handler

When a payment provider (like PawaPay) sends an asynchronous confirmation back to your local server, your endpoint must confirm the message is authentic and has not been processed already.
• What to verify in your server code:
	• Signature Verification: Check the cryptographic signature header (such as an HMAC-SHA256 signature using your shared webhook secret) before trusting the JSON body.
	• Status Verification: Double-check the transaction status directly against the payment provider's status API if needed, ensuring the status is genuinely COMPLETED before crediting a user's balance.
	• Idempotency: Store every processed depositId or transactionId in your database with a unique constraint so that replaying the same webhook payload twice cannot credit the balance multiple times.

4. Prevent Concurrency Flaws (Race Conditions)

When multiple requests arrive simultaneously, databases without proper locking can miscalculate balances.
• What to verify in your server code:
	• Atomic Database Transactions: Wrap balance checks and balance updates inside a single database transaction using row-level locking (e.g., SELECT ... FOR UPDATE in SQL) so two simultaneous withdrawal requests cannot read the same starting balance.
